  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tackling child labour in coffee supply chains in Vietnam</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2021, Rainforest Alliance introduced a new certification standard following its merger with UTZ, which placed strict emphasis on prohibiting child labor across certified farms. This requirement posed significant challenges for certificate holders, particularly smallholder farmers cultivating less than one hectare, who often struggle with low incomes and rely on children&#x2019;s labor.With primary funding from RVO and co-funding from two major coffee roasters, JDE and Lavazza, Rainforest Alliance launched a project involving 1,500 farmer households and their children. The project aimed to promote sustainable agriculture, safeguard children&#x2019;s rights, and eliminate child labor. It was managed by Rainforest Alliance and implemented by local consultant TMT Consulting in collaboration with Simexco (&#xd0;?k L?k) and Vinh Hiep (Gia Lai).Phase 1 (2021): The project focused on baseline assessments, gathering information from schools and local authorities to identify child labor practices.Phase 2 (2022&#x2013;2024): Activities concentrated on three areas:&#x2022;	Raising awareness of children&#x2019;s rights and child labor prevention through training, children&#x2019;s clubs, and community communication.&#x2022;	Strengthening farmers&#x2019; knowledge and management skills to improve household incomes.&#x2022;	Enhancing project management and outreach efforts.Results achieved:By the project&#x2019;s conclusion, no cases of child labor were reported among participating households. Farmer incomes increased by an average of 15% (Gia Lai 1.45% and Daklak 29.7%), contributing to improved livelihoods and resilience.Key lessons:1.	Close coordination with local authorities and schools proved critical for success.2.	Provide vocational training support for school dropouts over 15 years old and organize supplementary education for school dropouts under 15 years old.3.	Long-term sustainability requires integrating project activities with certification programs, such as maintaining Assess &amp; Address committees and mobile libraries to continue training and awareness after project closure.This initiative demonstrated how collaborative efforts among donors, industry partners, and local institutions can drive both social progress and sustainable agricultural development.</narrative>
  </description>
